    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2010 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2010-hl-sb-q8-b-i", topicId: "economics-3-5", conceptId: "effects-of-an-ageing-population",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2010 HL Section B Q8(b)(i)",
    stem: "By 2036 one in five Irish people will be over 65 years of age, with the greatest increase being in those over 80 years.",
    questionText: "Outline two possible economic effects of an ageing population on the Irish economy.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 5","answer":2,"ofParts":6,"perPart":5}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect — any two", marks: 10, contextNote: "Two effects, 2 at 5 marks. State: 2 marks. Explain: 3 marks. The marking scheme reprints this as 2046.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":5,"options":["Pressure on provision of state pensions: The government must encourage individuals through tax incentives to avail of private pensions in order to reduce the pressure on the government to provide state pensions.","Possible increased tax burden: with larger numbers of people over 65 the dependency ratio may increase resulting in the need for higher taxes on the workforce to fund services for older people.","Increased government expenditure: the government may spend a greater proportion of its revenue on the provision of services for the elderly such as; medical care, free transport, nursing homes.","Changing pattern of demand: demand for those goods and services required by older people will increase e.g. nursing homes; medication etc.","The participation rate falls: as more people reach retirement age, the supply of labour may be affected. Some may wish to work part-time. Some may retire.","Reduced mobility of labour: as people get older they are less like to move to a different location in search of work."]} },
    ],
