    year: 2012, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2012 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2012-hl-sb-q4-b", topicId: "economics-1-2", conceptId: "where-a-firm-locates",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2012 HL Section B Q4(b)",
    questionText: "Discuss the economic factors which should be considered by a firm when deciding where to locate its operations.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"5 @ 5","answer":5,"ofParts":15,"perPart":5}, totalMarks: 25,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A factor — any five", marks: 25, contextNote: "Five factors, 5 at 5 marks each. The last within countries is the scheme’s “Other”.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":5,"options":["Within countries — Transport infrastructure Firm may require good efficient transport infrastructure to get their goods to the market, source raw materials etc. This may include a good road network and possible closeness to a seaport and/or airport.","Within countries — Access to water supplies / power A manufacturing firm will require the uninterrupted supply of power and the supply of clean water.","Within countries — Land for expansion / cost of land If the firm plans to extend it will require land at a reasonable price for expansion.","Within countries — Planning permission Firms must ensure that appropriate planning permission is available from the local authority.","Within countries — Environmental regulations A particular industry may be subject to certain environment regulations and these must be checked with the appropriate authority.","Within countries — Workforce availability The firm will require a skilled / English speaking workforce.","Within countries — Government incentives There may be tax incentives / grants towards location available if the firm locates in certain areas of the country. The rate of Corporation Profits Tax in Ireland is one of the reasons why MNCs locate here. Other incentives may be available to attract firms i.e. less restrictive planning permission etc.","Within countries — Proximity to the market A firm which relies on a steady flow of consumers must locate close to the market i.e. a boutique.","Within countries — Proximity to the raw materials A firm which requires the usage of large amount of raw materials must locate close to the availability of these e.g. a power generating station.","Within countries — Other: Availability of social infrastructure / availability of back-up services (insurance / banking etc) / Government regulatory framework (bureaucratic red-tape, regulations).","Across Countries — Access to EU market / Member of the euro currency Firms will locate where they have access to free movement of their goods within the EU. Membership of the euro makes payment for international transactions within the euro zone easier.","Across Countries — Good industrial relations Firms want industrial peace so that production can continue uninterrupted by industrial action.","Across Countries — Attractiveness of return on investments Firms will locate where the rate of return on their investment is sufficiently good to attract them to a particular location.","Across Countries — Stable economic climate / economic growth Countries that are performing well / are experiencing economic growth offer increased business confidence, larger domestic markets and this may encourage firms to locate there.","Across Countries — Low wage / production costs Firms may locate to regions where wage rates are low and / or production costs are low."]} },
    ],
