    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2014 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2014-hl-sb-q6-a-ii", topicId: "economics-3-1", conceptId: "conflicting-aims",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2014 HL Section B Q6(a)(ii)",
    questionText: "Outline two possible conflicts that could arise between some of these Government aims/objectives.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 5","answer":2,"ofParts":4,"perPart":5}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A conflict — any two", marks: 10, contextNote: "Two conflicts, 5 marks each. Other acceptable answers marked on their merits.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":5,"options":["Sustainable government finances vs. economic growth/employment growth Government policies to increase employment may cause an increase in government spending and a resultant budget deficit. This may be short term though as an increase in employment will lead to a higher level of tax revenue in the future thus reducing the deficit.","Sustainable government finances vs. regional development Huge capital expenditure required (broadband and services such as hospitals and schools) to develop regionally, adversely affecting the budget deficit.","Full employment vs. equality Achieving equality means that the government has to divert resources from one section of society to another. The gains made towards equality may be made at the expense of reduced efficiency and lower employment. e.g., a tax on employers may result in fewer workers being employed.","Economic Growth vs. just social policy"]} },
    ],
