    questionText: "Explain, with the use of a fully labelled diagram, the long-run equilibrium position of a monopoly firm.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"11 + 8","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 19,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-labels", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "1 mark per label = 11 marks Price Quantity P1 Q1 C1 E D/AR MR MC AC SNP", marks: 11, contextNote: "The diagram, 1 mark for each of the 11 labels: the two axes, P1, Q1, C1, E, D/AR, MR, MC, AC and the supernormal profit area. The official diagram is shown in the figure.\n\nIrish solution labels: CI = marginal cost (MC), II = marginal revenue (MR), and MCH = average cost (AC). Use the same curve positions as in the English solution.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":11,"perOption":1,"options":["Price","Quantity","P1","Q1","C1","E","D/AR","MR","MC","AC","SNP"]} },
    { id: "r-explanation", kind: "point", verbatim: "• Equilibrium is at point E (where MC=MR). • Firms produce quantity Q1. • They sell the output at price P1. • Costs are at the level C1 (Y). • Firms earn supernormal profits as AR is greater than AC. • Inefficiency occurs as costs are not at the minimum point of the AC curve.", marks: 8, contextNote: "The explanation, 8 marks: equilibrium where MC = MR, output Q1 sold at P1 with costs at C1, supernormal profit because AR is above AC, and inefficiency because output is not at the lowest point of AC.", openList: true },
    ],
