    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2022 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2022-hl-q13-c-ii-yes", topicId: "economics-3-1", conceptId: "taxing-all-firms-at-15-percent-for",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2022 HL Q13(c)(ii) — yes",
    stem: "The Irish Government has signed up to the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) plan to reform global tax rules. The reforms will mean firms in Ireland that have an annual revenue of €750 million or more will be charged corporation tax at the rate of 15%, all other companies will be charged at the rate of 12.5%.",
    questionText: "Should the Irish Government impose the new 15% corporation tax rate on all firms in Ireland regardless of their annual turnover (including firms with annual turnover of less than €750 million)? Justify your answer. Answer for: YES.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 8","answer":1,"ofParts":3,"perPart":8}, totalMarks: 8,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason to impose it on all firms — any one", marks: 8, contextNote: "One developed justification for the yes side, 8 marks (4 + 4). The scheme credits both sides; these are its examples, not a closed list.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":8,"options":["Greater tax revenue for Irish government. If all firms were charged a higher rate on their profits it would lead to a higher tax take for the Irish government allowing for provision of better state services.","More stability in the tax revenue collected. The government could rely on a more consistent flow of revenue as the tax revenue collected form MNCs is volatile.","A fairer taxation system. If more tax revenue is collected form corporation tax, then maybe the government could change rates of income tax; VAT; excise duties – making the tax system fairer for the majority of taxpayers."]} },
    ],
