    id: "econ-2022-hl-deferred-sa-q5-a", topicId: "economics-4-2", conceptId: "irelands-comparative-advantage-for-data-centres",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2022 HL Section A Q5(a) — Deferred sitting",
    stem: "Ireland has one of the fastest growing data centre markets in Europe. Currently, most data centres in Ireland are located in Dublin with others in Cork, Limerick and Galway. (A data centre is a remote place, with many servers, used for the storage, processing and distribution of information.)",
    questionText: "Outline two sources of comparative advantage that Ireland offers, which helps to attract data centres in particular.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"4 + 3","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 7,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A source of comparative advantage — any two", marks: null, contextNote: "Two sources: the first correct one 4 marks and the second 3.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":4,"options":["Climate – Ireland has a cool temperate climate. This is an ideal climate to attract data centres as these large storage facilities would require larger investment in cooling systems in warmer climates. This acts as a source of comparative advantage.","Educated/Skilled workforce – Data centres require a highly skilled workforce to operate them and Ireland, with its high level of skilled workforce, provides this for companies wishing to set up here.","Low taxes on profits – Companies operating in Ireland currently pay corporation tax at a rate of 12.5%. This low rate of tax is a source of comparative advantage and helps to attract many data centres to Ireland.","Access to EU markets – By locating in Ireland which is in the EU, companies have access to EU markets which has a market close to 500 million people."],"perOptionSteps":[4,3]} },
    ],
