    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2022 Higher Level, deferred examination — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2022-hl-deferred-q13-b-ii", topicId: "economics-2-1", conceptId: "effects-of-a-living-wage",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2022 HL Q13(b)(ii) — Deferred sitting",
    stem: "The Low Pay Commission will examine how Ireland can move towards a living wage of €12.90, following authorisation by the government. This is different than the national minimum wage.",
    questionText: "Outline one possible economic effect on employees, businesses, and the government if Ireland did introduce the living wage of €12.90.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"7 + 7 + 7","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 21,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-employees", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect on employees — any one", marks: 7, contextNote: "Employees: one effect, 7 marks (4 + 3).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":7,"options":["Increased standard of living – if the government introduced the living wage, employees would have a greater level of disposable income and this would improve their standard of living.","Greater choice - employees would be able to buy more goods and services and this would give them more choice.","Incentive to work – the introduction of a living wage over the minimum wage would act as an incentive to work. The difference between the living wage and social welfare payments would be increased (also acceptable for business below)"]} },
    { id: "r-businesses", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect on businesses — any one", marks: 7, contextNote: "Businesses: one effect, 7 marks (4 + 3). The scheme also accepts “Incentive to work”, listed under employees, here.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":7,"options":["Increased costs – if the government introduced the living wage, businesses would be forced to pay this wage. This would represent an increase in businesses costs.","Reduced profits – the increase in businesses costs would almost certainly mean that profits would be reduced.","Possible reduction in staff numbers – if the business is forced to reduce costs it may do this by reducing the size of its workforce."]} },
    { id: "r-government", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect on the government — any one", marks: 7, contextNote: "Government: one effect, 7 marks (4 + 3).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":7,"options":["More direct tax revenue – if more people are working (incentive to work) and more people are earning higher wages the government should be taking in more direct tax (PAYE and USC).","More indirect tax revenue – if more people are working and earning higher wages, the government should take in more indirect tax (VAT) as more people have more disposable income.","Increased government current expenditure – wages for some state employees will rise and the government must pay for this.","Better redistribution of income – if people on lower pay receive increased pay then this helps the government achieve its policy of redistributing income more equitably."]} },
    ],
