    id: "econ-2023-hl-sa-q7-b", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "why-a-vacant-property-tax",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2023 HL Section A Q7(b)",
    stem: "The introduction of the vacant property tax in Ireland can be classed as a government intervention.",
    questionText: "Outline two reasons why the Irish government introduced this vacant property tax.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"6","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Increased government revenue to help with the homelessness/housing crisis.\n\nVacant properties represent a market failure: in a market where some consumers are unable to find a home to live in, vacant properties represent a waste of scarce resources.\n\nMore equitable: some vacant properties are holiday homes indicating that they are a second household for the owner. A progressive tax system should take a higher proportion away from owners of greater wealth.\n\nIncreased pressure on the Irish housing market year on year. Exacerbated further by the war in Ukraine.", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two reasons, 6 marks overall, with no published split; these are the scheme’s examples.", openList: true },
    ],
