    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2023 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2023-hl-q12-b-iii-adv", topicId: "economics-2-0", conceptId: "perfect-competition-advantage-and-disadvantage",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2023 HL Q12(b)(iii)",
    questionText: "Outline one possible economic advantage and one possible economic disadvantage of a perfectly competitive market.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal","notation":"10","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-advantage", kind: "point", verbatim: "Low prices - the firm sells its products at the lowest possible prices. (there is no exploitation of consumers).\n\nEfficiency is encouraged -the firm produces at the lowest point of the average cost curve so there is no waste of scare resources.\n\nNo advertising -the product is homogenous and there is perfect knowledge so there is no point is wasting scarce resources on advertising the firm and this saves consumers having to pay higher prices.\n\nConsumers are not exploited - as each firm only earn normal profit.\n\nFor firms – free entry and exit as they face no barriers to entry.", marks: null, contextNote: "One advantage. Complete BOTH headings. The scheme prices the pair as one 10: the first correct answer 6 marks and the second 4, with no fixed share for either heading.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-disadvantage", kind: "point", verbatim: "Little potential to expand or develop / no scope for economies of scale – as there are many small sellers, firms do not get large enough to benefit from economies of scale.\n\nLittle choice for consumers – as all products are homogeneous, there is little choice for consumers.\n\nLittle scope for research and development – lack of SNPs makes investment in R&D unlikely.", marks: null, contextNote: "One disadvantage. One disadvantage.", openList: true },
    ],
