    id: "econ-2024-hl-sa-q7-b", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "intervening-in-the-vaping-market",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2024 HL Section A Q7(b)",
    stem: "The number of teenagers in Ireland who vape is on the rise. Vaping poses a number of possible dangers to teenagers.",
    questionText: "Outline two ways the Irish government could intervene in the vaping market to overcome this market failure.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":5,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A way to intervene — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two ways, 3 marks each.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Ban sale of vapes to those under 18 years of age – the government could ban the sale of vapes to consumers under the age of 18 and enforce this law by imposing heavier penalties for sellers who breach the law.","Educational initiatives/information – the solution to information failure is better access to information. If the government allocates more funding to educating people about the harmful effects of vaping then consumers may be less likely to purchase vapes.","Impose higher taxation – the government could introduce higher levels of taxation and/or minimum unit pricing similar to the MUP of alcohol directed at making e-cigarettes more expensive to reduce the consumption of them.","Ban/restrict advertising for vape products – the government could legislate for strict advertising guidelines. This could restrict the marketing of vapes and consequently could have the effect of lowering demand.","Packaging / point of sale – the government could force producers to put health warnings on the packaging of products similar to that on cigarettes. They could insist on plain packaging and/or (as with cigarettes) restrict their sales to certain retailers or put then out-of-sight of people under 18."]} },
    ],
