    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2024 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2024-hl-q13-c-i", topicId: "economics-3-1", conceptId: "why-corporation-tax-receipts-rose",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2024 HL Q13(c)(i)",
    stem: "The diagram below shows Ireland’s Corporation Tax receipts between 2011 – 2022.",
    questionText: "Outline the overall trend in corporation tax receipts as shown in the diagram above, using data from the above line graph. Explain a reason for this trend.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"2 @ 4","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 8,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-trend", kind: "point", verbatim: "• Irelands corporation tax receipts have been increasing steadily year on year. • Between 2011 and 2022 they have increased by €19,123 million or by over 543%. • The biggest increase was from 2021 to 2022 when it increased from €15,324 million to €22,643 million.", marks: 4, contextNote: "Outline the rising trend with data from the graph, 4 marks: €3,520m in 2011 to €22,643m in 2022, an increase of €19,123m (over 543%). The scheme’s introduction says 2011 – 2021; the paper’s graph and the scheme’s own answer run to 2022.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-explanation", kind: "point", verbatim: "• This increase is due to the strong performance by many multinationals companies’ who are resident in Ireland primarily due to new MNCs being sent up in Ireland. • Ireland’s corporation tax rate being at an historic low rate of 12.5% since 1998.", marks: 4, contextNote: "Explain one reason for the trend, 4 marks. The scheme dates the 12.5% rate to 1998; Revenue’s 2003 Statistical Report gives 1 January 2003 for the standard 12.5% rate.", openList: true },
    ],
