    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-sa-q2-c", topicId: "economics-1-5", conceptId: "near-zero-marginal-cost-of-streaming",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2026 HL Section A Q2(c)",
    questionText: "Outline one reason why large online streaming companies, such as Spotify, can operate at almost zero marginal cost every time a new user subscribes.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 6","answer":1,"ofParts":4,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason marginal cost is near zero", marks: 6, contextNote: "One developed reason, 6 marks. Answer either (b) or (c).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Digital nature of streaming – music streaming is a digital service. Once the platform and music catalogue are created and licensed, supplying the same digital music to a new user does not need the production or distribution of another physical product. As a result, the cost of serving one extra subscriber is close to zero.","High fixed costs but very low marginal costs – streaming companies face high fixed costs, such as developing the platform / licensing music / maintaining servers. These costs do not increase significantly when a new user subscribes, which means the cost of serving a new user is almost zero.","Automated delivery of the service – the streaming service is delivered automatically through software and algorithms. Once the system is in place, no extra labour is required when a new user subscribes. Labour costs do not rise with each extra user.","Scalable technology and infrastructure – streaming platforms use scalable digital infrastructure that can handle large numbers of users at very low additional cost. This means the marginal cost of serving one more user remains minimal."]} },
    ],
