    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-sa-q4-b", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "raising-demand-for-second-hand-clothing",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2026 HL Section A Q4(b)",
    stem: "The global market for used clothes is now worth approx. $100bn, up from approx. $35bn in 2020. Second-hand clothes sales will account for 10% of the global clothing market in 2025.",
    questionText: "Suggest two possible measures the Irish government could introduce to increase demand for second-hand clothing.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":9,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A government measure — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two measures, 3 marks each. Answer either (b) or (c).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Reduce / Remove VAT on second-hand clothing – reducing or removing VAT on second-hand clothing would reduce the price for consumers. This would make second- hand items even cheaper compared to new clothes and would directly increase quantity demanded.","Introduce environmental taxes – environmental taxes on fast-fashion items would raise the price of such new clothing. This would make second-hand clothing relatively cheaper and increase demand through the substitution effect.","Launch public awareness / information campaigns – government campaigns could highlight the environmental and financial benefits of buying second-hand clothing. Such information may change consumer preferences and reduce any stigma with second-hand clothes.","Increased regulation on fast-fashion clothing – stricter government regulation on fast- fashion clothing, such as limits on waste, durability requirements, or environmental standards, may reduce the availability of fast-fashion clothing. Reduced availability of new clothing may encourage consumers to switch to second-hand clothing, which would increase its demand.","Government-supported school uniform swap schemes – if the government promoted or mandated school uniform swap days, it may normalise the use of second-hand clothing from a young age. Increased familiarity and social acceptance may reduce any stigma and change consumer preferences towards second-hand clothing over time.","Subsidies or grants for second-hand shops and repair services – the government could offer financial support to improve the quality, reliability, and presentation of second- hand clothing / to improve access to repair services. Greater confidence in the quality and durability of second-hand clothing may encourage more consumers to purchase them.","Financial support for online second-hand platforms – the government could support digital second-hand marketplaces through grants or training schemes. Improved accessibility and convenience may encourage more consumers to purchase second- hand clothing.","Government procurement of second-hand clothing – the government could increase the use of second-hand or recycled clothing in public services, such as uniforms / workwear. This would directly increase demand for second-hand clothing and help normalise its use.","Introduce green labelling or certification – the government could create an official eco- label for second-hand clothing. It would highlight the positive environmental impact of wearing used clothing and may make consumers more confident and willing to buy these products."]} },
    ],
