    questionText: "The data in the charts above create economic concerns for EU governments. What in your opinion are two economic concerns presented by the above data for EU governments.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":7,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A concern for EU governments — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two concerns, 3 marks each.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Slower economic growth relative to the USA – the data shows that EU GDP increased at a much slower rate than US GDP over the period. This raises concerns about the EU’s long-term economic performance and its capacity to generate sustained economic expansion.","Reduced global economic influence – as the USA economy grows more rapidly and becomes significantly larger, the EU’s relative influence in global trade, financial markets and international economic decision-making may weaken.","Reduced attractiveness for foreign direct investment (FDI) – stronger economic growth in the USA may make it a more attractive location for multinational firms. Slower growth in the EU could reduce inward investment, affecting employment and tax revenues.","Labour market and living standards concerns – slower economic growth may limit job creation across the EU. This can slow income growth and improvements in living standards for households.","Pressure on public finances and debt sustainability – weaker GDP growth can reduce tax revenues for EU governments while demand for public spending remains. Slower growth also makes it more difficult to manage and service national debt levels.","Innovation and technology gap concerns – faster US economic growth may reflect stronger performance in high-growth sectors such as technology and digital industries. This raises concerns that the EU could fall behind in innovation and productivity growth.","Growing economic gaps between EU member states – slower overall EU growth may hide uneven performance across countries. Some member states experienced significant economic growth in 2025 (e.g. Ireland and Poland) while other economies stagnated (e.g. Germany). Such differences may lead to widening gaps in incomes, employment, and living standards within the EU."]} },
    ],
