    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-sa-q10-a", topicId: "economics-3-3", conceptId: "effects-of-higher-prices",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2026 HL Section A Q10(a)",
    stem: "The diagram below compares the prices of consumer goods and services across Europe in 2024. Prices are presented as an index with the EU average = 100. The chart above shows that the average price of goods and services in Ireland was over 38% higher than the EU average in 2024.",
    questionText: "Outline two economic effects of higher prices on the Irish economy.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":12,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect of higher prices — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two effects, 3 marks each.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Reduces consumer purchasing power – higher prices reduce the real value of household incomes. Consumers can afford fewer goods and services, which reduces consumer demand in the Irish economy.","Reduces real value of savings – higher prices erode the purchasing power of money held in savings when the inflation rate exceeds interest rates. This discourages saving and reduces household financial wealth.","Reduces real burden of debt – inflation reduces the real value of loan repayments when interest rates are fixed (e.g. fixed rate mortgages). This makes debt easier to repay.","Increases wage demands / labour costs – rising prices may lead workers to seek compensatory wage increases to maintain living standards. This increases labour costs for firms and may reduce profit levels.","Weakens international competitiveness – higher domestic prices may make Irish goods and services more expensive relative to those produced in other countries. This reduces Ireland’s export competitiveness / weakens Ireland’s balance of payments position.","Reduces levels of tourism – Ireland becomes a more expensive destination for foreign visitors. Higher accommodation, food and service prices may reduce tourism inflows and associated revenue.","Increases government expenditure – higher prices may lead to greater government spending on indexed social welfare payments, public sector wages, and wider social supports.","Increases pressure on business profit margins – firms may face higher operating costs (e.g. energy, rent). If these cannot be fully passed on to consumers, profit margins decline.","Increases inflation expectations – persistent higher prices may lead households and firms to expect further inflation. This influences wage negotiations and price-setting behaviour.","Reduced domestic investment – higher costs and economic uncertainty may discourage firms from expansion / capital investment.","Increases income inequality – inflation disproportionately affects lower-income households who tend to spend a higher share of their income on essential / necessity goods and services. As a result, their real living standards decrease by more than higher-income households.","Increased administrative / pricing costs for firms – persistent inflation may require firms to change their prices more frequently. This creates additional costs such as reprinting menus, updating price lists, and changing labels."]} },
    ],
