    id: "econ-2026-hl-sa-q10-b", topicId: "economics-3-3", conceptId: "unequal-effects-of-food-price-rises",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2026 HL Section A Q10(b)",
    stem: "The diagram below compares the prices of consumer goods and services across Europe in 2024. Prices are presented as an index with the EU average = 100. The chart above shows that the average price of goods and services in Ireland was over 38% higher than the EU average in 2024.",
    questionText: "Outline two ways in which rising food prices do not affect all consumers equally.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":8,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A way consumers are affected unequally — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two ways, 3 marks each.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Share of income spent on food differs / Engel’s Law – lower-income households spend a higher proportion of their income on food. Rising food prices therefore place a greater financial burden on them compared to higher-income households.","Fixed income vulnerability – consumers reliant on fixed incomes, such as pensioners, may see no immediate increase in income as food prices increase. This reduces their purchasing power more severely.","Household size differences – larger households purchase greater quantities of food. Therefore, higher food prices lead to a larger overall increase in spending by larger households.","Ability to substitute to cheaper food options – some consumers can switch to own- brand goods or cheaper food substitutes when prices rise. However, others may have less flexibility due to preferences, availability, or nutritional needs.","Access to bulk buying and discount retailers – higher-income households may be better able to buy in bulk or travel to lower-cost supermarkets. Lower-income consumers may lack transport or cash flow to avail of such savings.","Urban versus rural price differences – consumers in rural or remote areas may face higher food prices due to transport costs and fewer retail outlets. Price increases may therefore affect them more severely.","Dietary and medical requirements – consumers with allergies, intolerances, or medical dietary needs may have a limited ability to switch to cheaper food options. This leaves them more exposed to price increases.","Reliance on food assistance or subsidies – households receiving supports such as school meals programmes may be partially protected from rising food prices, however consumers not eligible must bear the full impact of cost increases."]} },
    ],
