    id: "econ-2026-hl-q11-c-ii-disagree", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "tax-relief-for-gym-memberships",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q11(c)(ii) — disagree",
    stem: "Recently, some politicians proposed that gym memberships should qualify for tax relief.",
    questionText: "Do you agree or disagree, that gym memberships should receive tax relief? Indicate your opinion below by ticking (✓) the relevant box and justify your choice. Justify the choice: DISAGREE.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 6","answer":1,"ofParts":10,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason against tax relief on gym memberships", marks: 6, contextNote: "One developed justification for the disagree position, 6 marks. The scheme credits both positions; these are its examples, not a closed list.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Reduces government tax revenue / Creates opportunity cost – tax relief would reduce income tax receipts for the Irish exchequer. This would create opportunity costs by reducing available funding for public services such as healthcare, education and infrastructure.","Creates inequity in the tax system – higher-income workers are more likely to afford gym memberships and pay sufficient tax to benefit from relief. Lower-income groups may gain little despite facing greater barriers to participation. This makes the proposed policy regressive.","Limited impact on increasing participation – many existing gym members may receive relief without increasing their participation (a deadweight loss). Reduced membership prices do not guarantee regular gym attendance, which means the policy may not significantly improve health levels or reduce demand for healthcare services.","Direct funding more effective than tax relief – the tax relief supports private gym use on a broad basis rather than targeting government funds toward inactive or at-risk groups. Increasing funding to public health programmes or community sports initiatives may encourage more citizens to be active. Gym memberships already benefit from a reduced VAT rate of 9%, so further tax relief may only duplicate existing State supports rather than increasing participation.","May increase gym membership prices – gyms may increase membership prices if tax relief increases demand, as they would know consumers are receiving financial support. This would reduce the benefit passed on to members and limit the policy’s effectiveness. Some of the financial benefit from tax relief may accrue to gym operators rather than members.","Limited impact in rural areas with poor gym access – citizens in rural areas with fewer gyms may benefit less from the tax relief. This limits the national effectiveness of the policy.","Discriminates against other forms of exercise – citizens who participate in free or low-cost physical activity, such as running, walking, or community sport, would receive no tax benefit despite also contributing to improved public health.","May crowd out participation in community sport – subsidising gym memberships may encourage some citizens to move away from voluntary or community-based sport, i.e. weakening participation in local clubs.","Creates new administrative costs – implementing the tax relief would require checks on gym eligibility / verification of claims, which would increase administrative costs for Revenue and the government.","May create pressure for relief on other lifestyle activities – tax relief for gym memberships may lead to demands for similar supports for other leisure or wellness activities (e.g. yoga studios, golf clubs, wellness retreats etc.), which increases pressure on public finances."]} },
    ],
