    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-q12-c-ii-option-a", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "carbon-tax-or-renewable-subsidies",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q12(c)(ii) — option A",
    stem: "According to the Energy Institute, fossil fuels accounted for 81.4% of overall energy consumption in Ireland in 2024, an increase of 0.7% on the 2023 figure.",
    questionText: "In your opinion, which government intervention option would be more effective in reducing Ireland’s use of fossil fuels? • Option A — A larger annual increase in the carbon tax • Option B — Subsidies for renewable energy alternatives (e.g. home insulation, heat pumps, solar panels, electric vehicles etc.) Indicate your opinion below by ticking (✓) the relevant box and justify your opinion. Justify the choice: OPTION A.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 6","answer":1,"ofParts":8,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A justification for a larger carbon tax increase", marks: 6, contextNote: "One developed justification for the option A position, 6 marks. The scheme credits both positions; these are its examples, not a closed list.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Directly reduces demand for fossil fuels – an increase in the carbon tax increases the price of fossil fuels such as petrol, diesel and home heating oil. Increased prices discourage consumption and lead to a contraction in quantity demanded. This leads to an immediate reduction in fossil fuel usage across households and firms. Demand may be relatively price inelastic in the short term as households cannot easily switch their source of energy. However, in the long run, consumers and firms can adapt by investing in renewable alternatives, which would increase the effectiveness of the carbon tax.","Reflects the true social cost / market failure – fossil fuel consumption generates negative externalities such as carbon emissions and climate change. Pollution is not included in the price of products, so too much of the product is produced and consumed. A higher carbon tax would include such social costs into the market price and force consumers and firms to pay the true social cost of pollution. This encourages reduced fossil fuel use and more responsible behaviour.","Investment incentive for firms – increased costs for fossil fuels may incentivise firms to invest in cleaner production methods and to become more energy efficient. Businesses may switch to renewable inputs or electrified machinery to remain competitive.","Economy-wide impact – the carbon tax applies to transport, heating, electricity generation and industry in general. Its wide reach ensures less use of fossil fuels occurs across the entire economy rather than in isolated sectors.","Improves public health – a larger annual increase in the carbon tax would reduce the burning of fossil fuels. This improves air quality, which leads to an improvement in public health.","Encourages behavioural change in daily consumption patterns – increased fossil fuel prices encourage households to change their everyday behaviour, such as reducing car journeys, reducing home heating, or switching to public transport. These changes reduce fossil fuel use even before long-term investments are made.","Provides government revenue to fund decarbonisation – carbon tax revenue provides the government with funds to invest in renewable infrastructure, improved public transport, and national retrofitting schemes. This investment is necessary to increase Ireland’s supply of renewable energy, and to reduce its reliance on fossil fuels over time. The revenue could be redistributed to protect low-income households from higher energy costs.","Fairer for those families who can’t afford renewable alternatives – if they are compensated for the higher carbon tax paid through increases in social welfare."]} },
    ],
