    id: "econ-2026-hl-q13-a-ii", topicId: "economics-3-4", conceptId: "demand-for-credit",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q13(a)(ii)",
    stem: "60,770 personal loans worth €683 million were drawn down in Ireland in Q1, 2025. This was an annual increase of over 20%.",
    questionText: "Outline two factors, other than interest rates, which may increase the demand for credit/loans in the Irish economy.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 6","answer":2,"ofParts":10,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 12,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A factor increasing demand for credit — any two", marks: 12, contextNote: "Two factors, 6 marks each. Interest rates are excluded by the question.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":6,"options":["Economic growth – when the Irish economy grows, incomes and business profits rise. Households borrow to finance major purchases such as cars or home improvements, while firms borrow to expand production. Increased economic activity leads to greater demand for credit.","Increased consumer confidence – when households feel secure in their employment and optimistic about their future earnings, they are more willing to take on debt. Greater confidence leads to increased borrowing for goods and services.","Inflation / Cost of living pressure – when prices increase, households may borrow to maintain their standard of living. Firms may also require larger loans because production costs increase. This can lead to an increased demand for credit.","Population growth – an increase in population increases demand for housing, cars and household goods. This can lead to more borrowing, especially in a country like Ireland with a chronic shortage of housing.","Ease of access to credit – online banking and simplified application processes, and faster approval make borrowing quicker and more convenient. This results in an increase in the demand for loans.","Increase in asset prices – when house prices or share prices rise, homeowners may feel wealthier and more financially secure. This may encourage borrowing for investment, renovations, or consumption, which increases demand for credit.","Speculative motive – due to an expected increase in asset prices people may demand credit/loans to buy an asset they don’t own yet in the expectation of a higher future return.","Government policies / incentives – tax reliefs, grants, or investment schemes introduced by the government may encourage households and firms to spend or invest, e.g. the Home Energy Upgrade Loan Scheme. This can increase the demand for credit to finance these activities.","Marginal efficiency of capital – if firms expect good profits from investing in new equipment, technology, or expansion, they are more willing to borrow more to fund them. This positive outlook on returns increases the demand for credit.","Tax reductions – a reduction in income tax or VAT increases disposable income. This may encourage additional spending which is partly financed through credit."]} },
    ],
