    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-q15-b-iii-agree", topicId: "economics-3-1", conceptId: "hospitality-vat-reduction",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q15(b)(iii) — agree",
    stem: "The government plans to reduce value-added tax (VAT) for some of the hospitality sector from 13.5% to 9% in July 2026, at a cost of €681 million over a full year.",
    questionText: "Do you agree or disagree with this VAT reduction for some of the hospitality sector? Indicate your answer below by ticking (✓) the relevant box and justify your answer. Justify the choice: AGREE.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 6","answer":1,"ofParts":6,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason for the hospitality VAT cut", marks: 6, contextNote: "One developed justification for the agree position, 6 marks. The scheme credits both positions; these are its examples, not a closed list.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Supports employment in a labour-intensive sector – hospitality is labour-intensive. The reduction in VAT will reduce operating costs and may increase demand for services such as accommodation and restaurants. Increased demand may help to maintain or create jobs, especially in tourism-dependent areas.","Improves competitiveness of Irish tourism – the reduced VAT rate may help to reduce prices for tourists. This makes Ireland more competitive relative to other destinations and may increase visitor numbers and tourism revenue.","Eases cost pressures – many hospitality firms are experiencing high costs for energy, wages, and insurance. The VAT reduction will improve cash flow and profitability, which helps to prevent insolvencies / closures.","May reduce prices for consumers / tourists – if firms pass on the VAT reduction to consumers, prices for hospitality services such as meals, accommodation and hairdressing may fall. Lower prices increase purchasing power for domestic consumers and may also make Ireland a more competitive destination for tourists. This may increase visitor numbers and tourism revenue.","Supports regional economies – many hospitality businesses are located in rural and coastal areas. Supporting the sector may protect employment and income in regions with fewer alternative industries.","Stimulates economic activity in related sectors – increased tourism and hospitality activity may generate additional income and employment in other parts of the Irish economy, e.g. food producers, transport services, and entertainment businesses."]} },
    ],
