    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-q16-a-ii", topicId: "economics-1-0", conceptId: "why-hotel-prices-rise-for-events",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q16(a)(ii)",
    stem: "Prices for hotel rooms in Dublin rise sharply in the lead-up to large concerts / events.",
    questionText: "Explain why hotels can charge increased prices for their rooms during such concerts / events.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 7","answer":1,"ofParts":3,"perPart":7}, totalMarks: 7,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason hotels can charge more", marks: 7, contextNote: "One developed reason, 7 marks.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":7,"options":["Increased demand for hotel rooms / Dynamic pricing / Surge pricing – large concerts or events bring thousands of additional visitors to Dublin. These visitors need accommodation, which increases the demand for hotel rooms on specific dates. Hotels recognise the sharp surge in demand and often use dynamic pricing, which means they increase room prices as demand increases in order to maximise profit from the limited number of rooms available.","Capacity constraints / Fixed supply of hotel rooms in the short run – hotels in Dublin cannot suddenly make more rooms available when a major concert or event occurs. This results in fixed supply in the short run. When demand increases but supply is fixed / is inelastic / cannot increase, the market price of hotel rooms rise.","Greater willingness for attendees to pay / Inelastic PED among attendees – visitors travelling specifically to attend a concert or event may be willing to pay higher prices for accommodation because attending the event is important to them. Their demand is relatively price inelastic as they are willing to pay much more for a hotel room rather than miss out or stay farther away. When customers are willing to pay more, hotels can increase room prices without losing many bookings."]} },
    ],
