    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-q16-c-iii", topicId: "economics-4-0", conceptId: "malawi-and-falling-us-aid",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2026 HL Q16(c)(iii)",
    stem: "In January 2025 the United States (US) reduced its foreign aid programmes. It is estimated that US assistance to Malawi a LDC (Least Developed Country) may decline by around 59% in 2025 relative to 2024.",
    questionText: "Outline one economic challenge and one social challenge that Malawi may face as a result of this decline in US foreign aid.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"6 + 6","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 12,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-economic", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Economic challenge", marks: 6, contextNote: "Complete BOTH headings. One economic challenge, 6 marks.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Reduced government development spending – a decline in US foreign aid may mean fewer funds are available to the Malawian government for development projects such as infrastructure or agricultural programmes / reduced employment opportunities. This may slow economic development in Malawi.","Increased pressure on government finances – a significant reduction in US foreign aid may leave the Malawian government with fewer funds available in its budget. As a result, the government may need to increase borrowing or reduce spending. Increased borrowing may increase Malawi’s national debt and place further pressure on its public finances.","Reduced investment – foreign aid can help finance investment in infrastructure, agriculture, and industry in Malawi. A decline in US aid may reduce overall capital investment in the economy and limit future economic growth.","Reduced support for agriculture – foreign aid often funds fertiliser subsidies, irrigation schemes, or farming support programmes in developing countries like Malawi. A decline in US foreign aid may reduce support for farmers, which may reduce agricultural output and incomes in Malawi.","Reduced foreign exchange inflows – foreign aid provides foreign currency to LDCs such as Malawi. The large reduction in US aid may reduce the supply of foreign currency available in the country. This may make it harder for the country to pay for imports such as fuel, fertilisers, machinery, or medicines, which disrupts levels of production and trade."]} },
    { id: "r-social", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Social challenge", marks: 6, contextNote: "One social challenge, 6 marks.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Increased poverty – less US foreign aid may lead to reduced government spending and fewer aid-funded programmes in Malawi. This may reduce employment opportunities and incomes and make it harder for households to afford essentials such as food, housing, and healthcare.","Reduced funding for healthcare – a decline in US foreign aid may reduce funding for healthcare programmes in Malawi. This may reduce access to medical treatment and disease prevention programmes such as those targeting HIV/AIDS or malaria.","Reduced funding for education – US foreign aid may help support schools, teacher training, or educational programmes in Malawi. A decline in aid may reduce the quality or availability of education.","Reduced access to clean water and sanitation – foreign aid often supports programmes that improve access to clean water and sanitation in developing countries such as Malawi. A reduction in US foreign aid may limit funding for these programmes and worsen living conditions.","Increased food insecurity – foreign aid may support food assistance programmes or agricultural supports in Malawi. A decline in US aid may increase the risk of food shortages / food insecurity for vulnerable households."]} },
    ],
